I have installed two SEAGATE ST43400N drives on my Alpha
and have had no problems since I worked out the procedure.
Here is what I have done, I suspect that the approach for
your ST12400N is similar:

Procedure for installing Seagate disk drive on Alpha

1.  Determine what SCSI port numbers are in use,
and what ones are available.  You can do this by shutting down your
system ("shutdown -h now") and using the monitor to report the
used SCSI devices (show device).  "help show" should give you
the monitor options.  My system uses 3 and 4 for the internal
buss (buss 0), and I have installed Seagate drives on 2 and 5 on my
external buss (buss 1).
     
2.  Determine the logical unit number of your drive.
The 3000 model 400 has two scsi busses, 0 and 1.
Buss 0 is internal to the box, while buss 1 has
a SCSI connector on the back of the buss.
Multiply the buss number (1 in my case for the external SCSI) by 8
and add this to the SCSI port address that you have chosen for the disk
drive.  This gives me 10 and 13 for my two external disk drives.

3.  Change directory to /dev and execute the MAKEDEV command
with an argument containing the logical unit number.
This was "./MAKEDEV rz13" for me most recently.
"man MAKEDEV" will give you more information. 

4.  You should now be able to run the disklabel command
to see if you have a proper disk label: "disklabel -r rz13".
You probably don't, so...

5.  Make sure that you have an /etc/disktab entry for your
new disk drive.  My entry for one of my two external Seagate drives
looks like this:

rzxx|RZxx|ST43400N|st43400n|SEAGATE ST43400N:\
        :ty=winchester:dt=SCSI:ns#99:nt#21:nc#2736:\
        :oa#0:pa#131072:ba#8192:fa#1024:\
        :ob#131072:pb#262144:bb#8192:fb#1024:\
        :oc#0:pc#5688447:bc#8192:fc#1024:\
        :od#393216:pd#262144:bd#8192:fd#1024:\
        :oe#655360:pe#262144:be#8192:fe#1024:\
        :of#917504:pf#262144:bf#8192:ff#1024:\
        :og#1179648:pg#262144:bg#8192:fg#1024:\
        :oh#1441792:ph#4246655:bh#8192:fh#1024:
#    Second ST43400N:
rzxy|RZxy|ST43400N|st43400n|SEAGATE ST43400N:\
        :ty=winchester:dt=SCSI:ns#99:nt#21:nc#2736:\
        :oa#0:pa#131072:ba#8192:fa#1024:\

                      etc.   

You should be able to write a disk label now using the following command:

disklabel -w -r -n /dev/rrz13a rzxy

You can change the disk label after this by using other options to
disklabel.




6.  You can use the "scu" utility (see "man scu" for info)
to initialize your disk.  type 

scu -f /dev/rrz13c 

scu> show capacity
Disk Capacity Information:

                 Maximum Capacity: 5688447
                     Block Length: 512

scu> show nexus
Device Nexus: Bus: 1, Target: 5, Lun: 0, Device Type: Direct Access


7.  Next try to run "newfs /dev/rrz13c rzxy"
(using your own logical unit number, partition "c", 
and your own disk name (mine is rzxy here).

This didn't work for me.  An error message complained about
running out of space, even though I'm using the same size parameters
that worked for my other Seagate.  This suggests that the disk was
not labelled properly.  You may have to edit
the label using "disklabel -e rz13".
I changed the default sectors/track from 99 to 100,
sectors/cylinder to 2100,
and sectors/unit to 5688447 after much experimentation,
to obtain the maximum use of my disk.
