One of the things I found rather annoying about the whole "ris" approach to
installing software from a host onto a CD-less client is the fact that one
first has to copy everything from the distribution media to the ris area on
the host.  Other vendors (e.g. SGI) permit the installation to be done from
a mounted CD.  Perhaps a lot of people have hundreds of megabytes to spare
for their ris area, but we don't.  Why go through all the contortions (and
waste of disk space) when everything that one needs is already on the CD?

So, the following occurred to me:  How about doing something like this:

(1)  Run ris to setup the ris area, but copying only the minimal possible
amount of stuff from the CD.  Add clients as needed.  Then ...

(2)  Do		cd /var/adm/ris/ris0.alpha
		rm -rf product_001
		ln -s /mnt/ALPHA/BASE product_001
		mount /dev/rz4c /mnt

(3)  And finally, install the software from the client, directly from the CD.

The only possible problem I can see with this is the fact that the ris area's
"product_001" directory has a zero-length "checksums" file, which the CD does
not.  Other than that, everything else in the "product_001" directory is the
same as what is on the CD.

Any reason why this approach might not work?

