let's take the following situation (purely hypothetical, of 
course :-) ...

i am running dec osf/1 1.3 on a ds3000/300.

i have a huge, static, initialized array of error messages.

the final ld for my program barfs, complaining that 
	gp relocation out-of-range for small data or bss by,
	     <foo> in the positive direction,
	     <bar> in the negative direction.
	gp relocation out-of-range errors have occured and bad object file produced (corrective action must be taken)
and, oddly enough, if i zap most of the error message array 
this does not occur.

the various hardcopy documents (calling standard for axp systems,
programmer's guide) suggest that i should be able to use the -G
option, so beloved of MIPS/ULTRIX programmers, to adjust what's 
being stuck into the .sdata and .sbss segments.  however, i have 
noticed that
	(1) -G isn't documented in the cc or ld man pages 8-O
	(2) cc doesn't seem to pass it through (though it accepts
	the option)
	(3) osf/1 doesn't seem to provide -G 0 (no gp) libraries
	like MIPS/ULTRIX, which isn't really relevant but is rather
	suggestive that you aren't supposed to play gp games :-)

after a bit of playing with ld -G i *still* couldn't get a good 
object, so i did a cheesy hack -- i stuck the strings into the 
array as fixed-length char arrays of >8 bytes.  this is obviously
suboptimal in several respects.

what is the "right" way to deal with this?
