The VSZ is very large.  How big is your main memory?  Probably not more
than 71.2M.  The system has already given your proc 32MB.  How much
more is there to give it?  It sounds like your system is overcommitted.  
What other procs are running?

>Now the PAGEIN would seem to indicate that it's not actually crack
>that is causing the paging.  However, it seems clear to me that it is.
>Furthermore, using "ps -O RUSAGE -e" gives a list of all processes
>except kernel processes, and every single process has 0 in the PAGEIN
>column, which doesn't make much sense to me at all.

Yes, many of the vm stats reported by ps are bogus.  I've qar'd this.
I don't know if it will be fixed or not.  Perhaps you want to qar it 
via dec support.

>If anyone could suggest why the machine is paging with so much free
>memory, and why ps doesn't seem to think any of the processes are
>having PAGEINs, I'd be extremely grateful.  Does this look like an OS
>bug, or this something I'm missing here?

You don't have a lot of free pages.  vmstat shows that you have
around 80 free pages, which is about 640 kb, the amount of memory
in a dos box.  The stats show your system is overcommited (i.e
the demand for pages exceeds the supply) so paging is natural.

I don't remember if crack is i/o intensive or not, but you may
want to restrict the amount of memory the filesystem can use.
From /usr/sys/conf/param.c:

#if     !defined(UBCMINPERCENT)
int     ubc_minpercent = 10;
#else
int     ubc_minpercent = UBCMINPERCENT;
#endif

#if     !defined(UBCMAXPERCENT)
int     ubc_maxpercent = 100;
#else
int     ubc_maxpercent = UBCMAXPERCENT;
#endif

This means that the file system will retain 10% of main memory and can
grow to consume 100% of main memory.  You might want to tune the max
down a bit if you think that the vm and the file system are competing
for pages.

But, the one thing that would surely help is to add more memory or to
more disks to swap to.  Having more disks would allow multiple swap
operations to occur in parallel.  Since you have so much idle time,
it is almost a sure bet that increasing the performance of the i/o
system will result in increased throughput. 

