>purpose machine or it would be doggin' much more.  We put the machine up
>at the end of the semester so it'll be interesting to see what happens
>when we get back to 150 users logged in at once.

'find' is not cpu-intensive, it is i/o intensive.

The same can be said for most compilers.  Granted they have a fair
amount of CPU work to do, but much of their work is reading and writing
program and intermediary files.  Also, most compilers use large amounts
of virtual space, so the i/o system gets involved in this way as well
(supplying pages to the virtual memory system).

Keep in mind that it is the same turbochannel in the Alpha that was in
the 5000/200, yet the cpu puts at much larger load onto it.  I hope you
have reasonable disks (i.e. I hope you retired the rz5x's with the
5000/200 and bought rz2x's instead of rz7x's...) and that you spread
the load across the drives/controllers.

This isn't to say that there are no scheduler problems in the presence
of CPU bound jobs.  There was work released in 1.3 to address some of
the things people have reported.  It is just saying that the examples
you site indicate an i/o starved machine instead of a cpu starved
machine.  Examination of vmstat output when the system is heavily
loaded would be interesting.

