Subject: Problem with swap on OSF/1 V1.3
Message-ID: <GRUNWALD.94Jan1222058@foobar.cs.colorado.edu>
Sender: usenet@cnsnews.Colorado.EDU (Net News Administrator)
Nntp-Posting-Host: foobar.cs.colorado.edu
Reply-To: grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu
Organization: University of Colorado at Boulder
Date: Sun, 2 Jan 1994 05:20:57 GMT
Lines: 36


I have three Alpha 3000/400's, running OSF/1 V1.3.

Without having changing anything I can think of in the last 3 days,
I've suddenly started to experience swap problems. I have 96Mb memory,
145 Mb swap.

At various points, with no warning, my ``wired'' storage and the
reserved swap space (according to `syd' and `monitor') begin to climb.
Looking at syd & ps doesn't show any particular process as consuming
undue amounts of storage. In fact, things don't seem to change at all.

Eventually, all swap gets consumed and the system is wedged. I run in
swap over-commit mode, but these are essentially single-user machines,
and it only seems to occur on one.

Each time this occured, I was compiling C++ files using cxx under
Emacs. But, I do this all the time & have never encountered problems
before.

One possible problem I thought of was that I recently started using
``efence'', the electric fence memory allocation error-catcher. Efence
works by allocation mmap'd segments that cause access faults if you
access illegal memory regions. This sucks down VM space, but shouldn't
be using swap space (I would think). I've stopped using this, but have
encountered one problem since then, but I might have had efence linked
into an application at that time.

So,

(1) has anyone else encountered such behavior under OSF/1?

(2) does anyone know if efence causes problems on OSF/1?


Dirk Grunwald                          Asst. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder


