>>>>> "I" == ivie  <ivie@cc.usu.edu> writes:

I> I see this sort of thing whenever I do disk-intensive thingies. For example,
I> if you have a couple of really big files and execute a shell script to 
I> infinitely copy them to /dev/null, after a couple of minutes the machine is
I> pretty much gone. It's as if the disk cache takes over all of memory or 
I> something.

Well, it does!  DEC OSF/1 has a unified buffer cache, meaning that
memory pages and file data pages share the same allocation pool.  The VM
system in V1.2 (and, to a lesser extent, in V1.3) doesn't do a
spectacular job of choosing pages to eject from memory, so that reading
a large file can effectively flush out all the instruction/data pages.
